- id: harmony-design
  title: Applying a Scale
  audience: all
  summary: Compare C major and D major using relative pattern notes.
  prose: 'Before starting: Start with stopped channel 1 assigned pattern 1, range 16, attacks 1,5,9,13,
    channel Note 60/Vel 80/Len 1/2 and step 13 Note 62/Vel 50. A second channel can use pattern 2 with
    attacks 1,9, relative degrees 0,4 and octave −1. Apply C major. [Make a piece](#learn) chapters 2–6
    give the full preparation. If using only one channel, ignore the bass comparison.


    ### Restore note inheritance


    1. Select CH01 and open Masks.

    2. With no step held, set Note to X.

    3. Hold step 13 and press K2, then release.

    4. Select Vel, hold step 13, set 50 and release.


    All Note exceptions are cleared. K2 also cleared the velocity exception, which is deliberately restored.


    ### Retain the same melody in the pattern


    1. Tap Pattern (5,8) until Note is shown; select pattern 1 at (1,1).

    2. Tap (1,7), (5,7) and (9,7) for degree 0.

    3. Tap (13,6) for degree 1.

    4. Play two bars and stop.


    C–C–C–D returns through relative notes. The quieter ending stays in the channel mask.


    ### Prepare a second scale


    1. Open Scale (4,8).

    2. Select scale slot 2.

    3. Play two bars and stop to hear its stored C-major setting.

    4. Select Root with E2; set D with E3.

    5. Select Scale and choose Major.


    The melody and bass still use C major during the comparison. D major is now prepared in the
    draft; press K3 before using the grid.


    ### Compare the transposition


    1. Press K3 to apply D major.

    2. Play two bars and stop.

    3. Select the unchanged C-major slot 1 and press K3.


    The phrase becomes D–D–D–E and bass D–A. Rhythm and velocity are preserved. C major is restored for
    the arrangement.


    Resulting project: CH01 P1 degrees0/0/0/1; Note defaultX; step 13NoteX/Vel 50; Vel 80/Len1/2 defaults
    retained. CH02 unchanged. Applied C major restored.'
  musical_use: Repeat a root–third–fifth pattern over two scale slots. The rhythm remains the same while
    the harmony changes.
