- id: sequence-composition
  title: Assigning Patterns to Channels
  audience: all
  summary: Hear an independent bass pulse beneath the phrase.
  prose: 'Before starting: Use a 16-step CH01 phrase with pattern 1 assigned: attacks 1,5,9,13; channel
    Note 60, Vel 80, Len 1/2; step 13 Note 62 and Vel 50. Use C major at 90 BPM and Rate /1. Playback
    is stopped. [Make a piece](#learn) chapters 2–5 supply this project. An independent project with the
    same state also works; pattern 2 must be empty.


    ### Enter two bass notes


    1. Tap Pattern (5,8) until Trig is shown; select pattern 2 at (2,1).

    2. Add attacks at (1,4) and (9,4).

    3. Tap Pattern once to open Note.

    4. Tap (1,7) for degree 0 and (9,3) for degree 4.


    Pattern 2 supplies a root and fifth.


    ### Route channel2


    1. Tap Channel (3,8); select channel 2 at (2,1).

    2. Open Channel tasks → Device; select an independent output and press K3 to apply.

    3. Tap pattern slot 2 at (2,2).

    4. Hold (1,4), tap (16,4), release.

    5. Tap (9,8) to set channel octave −1.


    The bass uses MIDI 48 and55 in the default C-major scale. Both channels repeat in16 steps.


    ### Hear the two parts


    Play two bars and stop.


    C–C–C–D sounds above a C–G bass pulse. Both parts retain independent settings.


    ### Compare overlapping rhythms


    1. Select CH01 and temporarily assign pattern 2 alongside pattern 1.

    2. Open Merge modes. Compare Skip and All for Trig.

    3. Play and stop after each comparison.

    4. Unassign pattern 2 from CH01 before continuing.


    Skip removes overlapping attacks 1 and9, leaving5 and13. All restores the four attacks. The existing
    Note 60 default and step 13Note62 exception keep the pitch comparison fixed. Unassigning pattern 2
    returns the original phrase; CH02 keeps its bass pattern.


    Resulting project: CH01 remains C–C–C–D. CH02/P2 attacks 1/9, relative degrees0/4, range 16, octave−1,
    inherited notes/velocity, no masks.'
  musical_use: Assign the same pattern to bass and chord channels. Use different masks and lengths to
    distinguish the parts.
  related:
  - adding-patterns-to-channels
  - typical-workflow
  sources:
